K4 cross-review: kernel/src/trust.rs and kernel/src/trustselftest.rs
Reviewer: freebuff. Date: 2026-10-23.

Subject: commit ee3538a.  The two files at HEAD are byte-identical to ee3538a:
  $ git diff --quiet ee3538a HEAD -- kernel/src/trust.rs kernel/src/trustselftest.rs && echo identical
  identical to ee3538a
  $ git rev-parse HEAD:kernel/src/trust.rs HEAD:kernel/src/trustselftest.rs
  0e1e5a5c79a46f70d926a06665dc3ba607a4ea2b
  f682b793bbb84904fae1ebae2dc3adee240fe529

Environment: Windows Git Bash, rustc 1.98.1, QEMU 11.1.0 (SeaBIOS + OVMF),
OpenSSL 3.5.9 CLI, Python 3.13 with cryptography 50.0.1 (OpenSSL bindings).

Reviewer artefact committed with this log:
  docs/reviews/k4-freebuff-vectors.py
It parses the constants out of trustselftest.rs, re-runs the committed fixture
generator, checks RFC 8032 7.1 TEST 1-3 against OpenSSL, compares every SHA-2
constant to hashlib, and compiles the kernel's own trust.rs in a scratch crate
(wasm32-wasip1, run under node's WASI because this host's link.exe is broken) to
score the implementation against those oracles.


1. What the in-kernel gate actually runs
----------------------------------------
The gate is 18 checks, not the number the commit title suggests: 11 trust
checks and 7 process/capability checks.  Baseline serial, from the scratch copy:

  --- K4 in-kernel C2 trust and capability gate (per-process PML4, ring 0 only, no isolation yet)
    ok    sha256: empty string digest matches FIPS 180-4
    ok    sha256: multi-block fox digest matches FIPS 180-4
    ok    sha512: empty string digest matches FIPS 180-4
    ok    ed25519: OpenSSL vector 0 valid signature accepted
    ok    ed25519 negative: bit-flipped signature rejected
    ok    ed25519 negative: altered message rejected
    ok    ed25519 negative: wrong public key rejected
    ok    c2-manifest: OpenSSL-signed fixture manifest verified
    ok    c2-manifest negative: tampered manifest content rejected
    ok    package: matching ELF digest accepted
    ok    package negative: tampered ELF digest rejected
  sched:       scheduler initialized; PID 1 (novad) spawned
    ok    proc: PID 1 (novad) active
    ok    cap gate: novad granted declared fs:read
    ok    cap gate: novad granted declared compute:verify
    ok    cap gate: novad DENIED undeclared device:camera
    ok    proc: PID 2 active in process table
    ok    cap table: untrusted DENIED fs:read by capability table
    ok    cap table: untrusted DENIED device:camera by capability table

Note that the Ed25519 negatives are three: bit flip, altered message, wrong key.
There is no non-canonical-S case and no all-zero / small-order-key case, even
though trustselftest.rs:9 advertises "non-canonical S" as one of its checks:

  $ grep -n 'non-canonical\|S + L\|all-zero\|small-order' kernel/src/trustselftest.rs
  9://! 4. Ed25519 negative cases (signature bit flip, altered message, wrong key, non-canonical S).

That grep finds only the doc comment.  Section 5 below shows what that costs.


