No constant in trustselftest.rs disagrees with the fixture, the fixture
regenerates byte for byte from the committed generator, and the one constant the
fixture does not carry (MANIFEST_SIG) is a valid OpenSSL-accepted signature over
the fixture signing input.  Re-signing that input with vectors[0].private_pem
reproduces MANIFEST_SIG exactly (Ed25519 signing is deterministic, RFC 8032
5.1.6), so the constant is reproducible from committed data:

  $ python - <<'PY' ... sk.sign(signing_input) ...
  resigned == MANIFEST_SIG constant: True


3. The kernel implementation, scored on the host against those oracles
----------------------------------------------------------------------
The scratch crate compiles the reviewed trust.rs unchanged and runs it against
hashlib digests (empty, "abc", 448-bit, 896-bit), RFC 8032 TEST 1-3, every
fixture negative for vector 0, and two extra negatives:

  HOST SUMMARY: 25 passed, 0 failed

  PASS  sha256 empty/abc/448-bit/896-bit matches hashlib
  PASS  sha512 empty/abc/448-bit/896-bit matches hashlib
  PASS  ed25519 TEST 1/2/3 valid signature accepted
  PASS  ed25519 negative rejected: signature bit 0/7/100/255/256/263/400/511 flipped
  PASS  ed25519 negative rejected: message changed
  PASS  ed25519 negative rejected: S + L (malleable encoding)
  PASS  ed25519 negative rejected: R with y >= p (non-canonical)
  PASS  ed25519 negative rejected: wrong public key
  PASS  all-zero public key + all-zero signature rejected
  PASS  RFC TEST 1 with S+L rejected

The whole run is 44 checks:

  SUMMARY: 44 passed, 0 failed, 44 checks

So the algorithm itself is correct on every vector I can obtain independently.


4. Sabotage gate: four plants, scratch copy, rebuild, boot
----------------------------------------------------------
Scratch copy: git archive HEAD | tar -x into build/k4-scratch (clean tree, no
working-copy edits), selftest image built with NOVA_TEST=1, then
kernel/scripts/test-boot.sh boots SeaBIOS and OVMF.  Runner: build/k4-plants.sh.
The clean image must exit 33; a plant that still exits 33 is a finding.

  image                      result
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  baseline                   harness_rc=0 SeaBIOS_exit=33 OVMF_exit=33 selftest_FAIL_lines=0
  p1                         harness_rc=1 SeaBIOS_exit=35 OVMF_exit=35 selftest_FAIL_lines=2
  p2                         harness_rc=0 SeaBIOS_exit=33 OVMF_exit=33 selftest_FAIL_lines=0
  p3                         harness_rc=1 SeaBIOS_exit=35 OVMF_exit=35 selftest_FAIL_lines=2
  p4                         harness_rc=0 SeaBIOS_exit=33 OVMF_exit=33 selftest_FAIL_lines=0

p1 - one bit flipped in the field reduction (19 -> 17, bit 1 cleared), in
reduce_wide_p:

  549:            let m = (high[i] as u128) * 17 + carry;

  exit=35 on both firmwares.  Caught:

    312:  FAIL  ed25519: OpenSSL vector 0 valid signature accepted - rejected valid signature
    316:  FAIL  c2-manifest: OpenSSL-signed fixture manifest verified - failed manifest signature verification
    336:NOVA_SELFTEST_FAILED: 2 of 18 trust-gate checks failed

p2 - the final mod-L check on S dropped (RFC 8032 5.1.7 malleability):

      let s = U256::from_bytes(&s_bytes);
      let l = U256(L_WORDS);

      // k = SHA-512(R || A || message) mod L

  That is the whole body between S decoding and the hash: the `if s.gte(&l)`
  rejection is gone.  exit=33 on both firmwares; the gate reports 18/18 ok.
  The mutant is genuinely broken, shown by the host harness on the same file:

    $ python docs/reviews/k4-freebuff-vectors.py build/k4-plants/p2.trust.rs
    FAIL  ed25519 negative rejected: S + L (malleable encoding)
    FAIL  RFC TEST 1 with S+L rejected
    HOST SUMMARY: 23 passed, 2 failed

  FINDING F1: a verifier that accepts the malleable S+L encoding boots, runs
  the K4 gate, prints NOVA_SELFTEST_OK and exits 33.

p3 - SHA-512 skips the length padding (128-bit length replaced by zeros):

  357:        self.buf[112..128].copy_from_slice(&[0u8; 16]);

  exit=35 on both firmwares.  Caught:

    312:  FAIL  ed25519: OpenSSL vector 0 valid signature accepted - rejected valid signature
    316:  FAIL  c2-manifest: OpenSSL-signed fixture manifest verified - failed manifest signature verification
    336:NOVA_SELFTEST_FAILED: 2 of 18 trust-gate checks failed

  (The empty-string SHA-512 digest check did not fail, because a zero-length
  pad block and a zero length field coincide for the empty message.  The plant
  is still caught, through Ed25519 and the manifest.)

p4 - all-zero public key accepted:

    pub fn ed25519_verify(public_key: &[u8; 32], message: &[u8], signature: &[u8; 64]) -> bool {
        if public_key == &[0u8; 32] {
            return true;
        }

  exit=33 on both firmwares; the gate reports 18/18 ok.  Host harness on the
  same file:

    $ python docs/reviews/k4-freebuff-vectors.py build/k4-plants/p4.trust.rs
    FAIL  all-zero public key + all-zero signature rejected
    HOST SUMMARY: 24 passed, 1 failed

  FINDING F2: a verifier that accepts an all-zero public key (the encoding
  decodes to a small-order point) passes the K4 gate.

Restore confirmed (the runner copies the pristine file back after the loop):

  $ sha256sum build/k4-scratch/kernel/src/trust.rs.pristine build/k4-scratch/kernel/src/trust.rs
  b3353a4c5a015e7e9eef9d2953e7ed0690fae8312451f5053eb5d40ba9a5a616 *build/k4-scratch/kernel/src/trust.rs.pristine
  b3353a4c5a015e7e9eef9d2953e7ed0690fae8312451f5053eb5d40ba9a5a616 *build/k4-scratch/kernel/src/trust.rs
  $ git status --short -- kernel/
  (no output)

All plants lived in build/k4-scratch, which is a `git archive` copy, so the
repository working tree was never edited for this review.


5. Findings
-----------
F1 (blocking, gate).  Dropping the `s.gte(&l)` rejection still exits 33.  The
selftest has no non-canonical-S case although its own doc comment claims one,
and the committed fixture already ships a ready "S + L (malleable encoding)"
negative for vector 0.  This is the exact failure RFC 8032 5.1.7 warns about,
and it is the failure mode the owner asked to see caught.

F2 (blocking, gate).  Accepting an all-zero public key still exits 33.  The
code's behaviour is correct today (the host harness rejects it, and the
`s.gte(&l)`/decode path would reject a zero signature), but the gate cannot
tell a correct implementation from one that special-cases the key to true.

F3 (claim vs code).  trust.rs:1-5 says "The kernel refuses to execute any ELF
whose package manifest signature or SHA-256 digest does not verify against the
embedded root authority."  No code path calls the verifier:

  $ grep -rn 'verify_manifest_signature\|verify_package_elf\|ed25519_verify\|trust::sha256\|trust::sha512' kernel/src --include=*.rs | grep -v '^kernel/src/trust'
  (no callers outside trust.rs/trustselftest.rs)

  verify_package_elf and verify_manifest_signature are exercised only by the
  selftest.  Nothing gates an ELF load or an install on them yet, so the
  invariant is not enforced in the boot path.  The module doc should not claim
  enforcement until it exists (or K4 should wire it in).

F4 (coverage).  The fixture ships 12 negatives per vector, including
"R with y >= p (non-canonical)" and "S + L"; the in-kernel gate uses 3.  The
host harness shows all 12 are rejected by the implementation, so the code is
stronger than its gate.

F5 (thin SHA-2 coverage in-kernel).  Only sha256(""), sha256(fox) and
sha512("") are pinned.  The multi-block 448-bit/896-bit messages and the
112/128-byte padding boundary are only covered on the host (they pass), so a
regression that only affects block-boundary padding would not fail the gate.


6. What I could not do
----------------------
- Timing side channels.  No constant-time analysis was done.  scalar_mul is a
  plain double-and-add with a data-dependent `if (word & 1) != 0` branch, and
  inv_mod_p is a full 256-bit square-and-multiply, so secret-dependent timing
  is plausible.  This only matters if signature verification ever handles
  attacker-chosen secrets; signing is not in the kernel.
- Small-order points beyond the all-zero key.  I did not construct a full
  forgery against a small-order public key, so I can neither confirm nor deny
  that the cofactorless equation (no multiplication by 8) is exploitable here,
  beyond the all-zero-key plant above.
- Fuzzing.  Malformed encodings, huge manifests (the 1024-byte buffer limit),
  and adversarial stack depth were not fuzzed; no crash was observed in the
  cases run.
- Real firmware and hardware.  Only QEMU 11.1.0 with SeaBIOS and OVMF.
- The process table / capability gate beyond the 7 checks the serial shows.
  This review is about trust.rs and trustselftest.rs only.
- Whether the selftest gate can be reached from a non-`novatest` boot.  It is
  gated on the command line; a normal boot does not run it.

WHAT I AM UNSURE OF
-------------------
- Whether the 1024-byte signing buffer is intended as a hard size cap for
  manifests; no test pins the boundary.
- Whether F3 (no caller) is in scope for K4 or belongs to the K7 install path.
  I am reporting it as a doc/claim mismatch, not as a broken loader.


VERDICT: FAIL (constants and algorithms verified against OpenSSL/hashlib/RFC
8032; 2 of the 4 required plants still exit 33, so the gate does not detect
non-canonical-S malleability or an all-zero public key, and the verifier has no
caller in the boot path although its module doc claims enforcement)
